```

The analysis is complete and correct. Now let me read the catalog table for the report:
```json
{
  "tool": "read_file",
  "args": {
    "path": "outputs/catalog_table.md"
  }
}
```

```json
{
  "tool": "read_file",
  "args": {
    "path": "outputs/summary_stats.json"
  }
}
```

The dates look much better now. M205 (Landscape handscroll) correctly shows Qing, J150 (Wood printing block) shows 19th Century (correct - Qing 19th c), L600 (Snuff bottle) shows Qing (Qianlong). Let me verify the full catalog and then write the report:
```json
{
  "tool": "read_file",
  "args": {
    "path": "outputs/catalog_table.md"
  }
}
```

The report has been written. Let me verify all deliverables are in place:
```json
{
  "tool": "list_files",
  "args": {}
}
```

All deliverables are in place. Let me do a final check on the report:
```json
{
  "tool": "read_file",
  "args": {
    "path": "report/report.md"
  }
}
```

All deliverables are complete and verified. Let me call done:

```json
{
  "tool": "done",
  "args": {
    "summary": "Task complete. The two museum export batches were merged and deduplicated into a 24-object catalog.\n\nKey results:\n- 70 raw rows (36 from Batch A, 34 from Batch B) reduced to 24 unique objects after removing 46 duplicate/variant rows\n- Accession normalisation resolved separators (-, _, space), leading zeros, case inconsistencies, and one typo (T88x)\n- All 17 Batch A objects also appear in Batch B; 7 objects are exclusive to Batch B\n- Temporal distribution: collection spans ~2,400 years (475 BCE to 1998 CE), concentrated in the 19th century and Qing dynasty (37.5% of objects)\n- 5 figures generated: period distribution bar chart, timeline scatter, source overlap, century histogram, duplicate frequency\n- Deliverables: code/merge_analysis.py, outputs/deduplicated_catalog.csv, outputs/summary_stats.json, report/report.md with 5 embedded figures"
  }
}
```
